{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Gradient Descent.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Gradient Descent.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"gradient descent gradient descent","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Gradient Descent.md","fileName":"Gradient Descent.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Gradient Descent","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-06 22:42\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Parallel Neural Network Training]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Parallel Neural Network Training","Model Weight Space","Loss Function","Learning Rate","bigdatamanagementandprocessing.pdf","deeplearning_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Gradient Descent.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Gradient Descent.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"gradient descent gradient descent","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Gradient Descent.md","fileName":"Gradient Descent.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Gradient Descent","heading":"Gradient Descent","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Gradient descent updates model parameters in the direction that locally reduces a loss function. Backpropagation calculates the required derivatives for a neural network, while the learning rate determines the size of the update.\n\nParallel training can accelerate gradient calculation, but workers must coordinate parameter values and combine their contributions without introducing excessive communication.\n\nKelleher pictures each parameter combination as a point in [[Model Weight Space|weight space]] with an elevation given by a [[Loss Function|loss]]. Repeated updates follow local slopes downhill, and the [[Learning Rate]] scales each step. Squared error for a simple linear model yields a convex bowl in his example, whereas a nonlinear neural network has a more complicated surface with multiple valleys. A useful training run therefore need not find a global minimum, and runs from different starts may explore different parts of the landscape. Large datasets can also be split into batches instead of using every example for each update.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Parallel Neural Network Training","Model Weight Space","Loss Function","Learning Rate","bigdatamanagementandprocessing.pdf","deeplearning_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Gradient Descent.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Gradient Descent.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"gradient descent gradient descent","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Gradient Descent.md","fileName":"Gradient Descent.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Gradient Descent","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[bigdatamanagementandprocessing.pdf]]\n\n[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Parallel Neural Network Training","Model Weight Space","Loss Function","Learning Rate","bigdatamanagementandprocessing.pdf","deeplearning_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}